<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intolerable Acts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1774</span> · Also known as Coercive Acts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Intolerable Acts, called the Coercive Acts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, were four punitive laws passed by the British Parliament in 1774 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a>. They closed the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, curtailed self-government in Massachusetts and allowed the quartering of troops, and they provoked the colonies to coordinate their resistance through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts.jpg" alt="Intolerable Acts" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Trumbull · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_Independence_(1819),_by_John_Trumbull.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
